---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const title = 'How TTK Works in Delta Force – Formula | DF TOOLS';
const description =
  'How time-to-kill works in Delta Force: the TTK formula, how ammo and armor tiers change shots-to-kill, and worked examples you can reproduce in our calculator.';
const updated = '2026-10-08';

const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How TTK works in Delta Force',
  datePublished: '2026-10-08',
  dateModified: updated,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DF TOOLS' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DF TOOLS' },
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[articleLd]}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Guides' }, { name: 'How TTK Works' }]} />
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>How TTK works in Delta Force</h1>
    <div class="h1-bar"></div>
    <p class="lede">
      Time-to-kill looks like a simple division — until armor gets involved. Here is the full chain: fire rate to
      bullet interval, damage to shots-to-kill, and the tier-gap model that decides how much of your damage a
      plate actually eats.
    </p>
    <div class="microline"><span>By DF TOOLS</span><span>Updated {updated}</span></div>
  </div>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Quick answer</h2>
    <p>
      TTK = (shots to kill − 1) × 60,000 ÷ fire rate in RPM. Shots to kill is your target's health divided by the
      damage each shot deals after armor — and against armor, "damage after armor" depends on your ammo tier
      versus the target's armor tier, not just the number on the gun.
    </p>
    <div class="hazard">Do it now: run your exact gun through the <a href="/ttk-calculator/">TTK calculator</a> while you read.</div>

    <h2>Step 1 — fire rate to bullet interval</h2>
    <p>A gun firing 700 RPM puts a bullet in the air every 85.7 milliseconds:</p>
    <pre class="formula">interval (ms) = 60,000 ÷ RPM</pre>
    <p>The first shot is free — it happens the instant you press the button — which is why the formula subtracts one interval.</p>

    <h2>Step 2 — damage to shots-to-kill</h2>
    <pre class="formula">shots = ceil(health ÷ damage per shot)</pre>
    <p>Unarmored, that's the whole story: a 42-damage gun needs 3 shots on a 100-health operator. Armored, the damage per shot collapses:</p>
    <pre class="formula">damage per shot = dmg × (pen + (1 − pen) × blockedShare)</pre>
    <p>Where <code>pen</code> is the penetration chance from the ammo-vs-armor tier gap, and <code>blockedShare</code> is the slice of a blocked shot that still hurts (community estimate ≈ 30%). Same 42-damage gun with same-tier ammo: about 29 damage per shot reaches health — 4 shots instead of 3, and that assumes a fresh plate.</p>

    <h2>Step 3 — the plate is a second health bar</h2>
    <p>
      Blocked damage chips armor durability. A 50-point plate dies to a stream of blocked shots, and after it
      breaks, every bullet lands at full damage. That's why TTK against armor is a two-phase problem, and why a
      "half-dead" plate changes fights: the <a href="/ttk-calculator/">calculator</a> reports the exact shot on
      which armor breaks.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Shots</th><th>TTK @ 700 RPM</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>42 dmg, no armor</td><td class="mono">3</td><td class="mono">171 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>42 dmg vs same-tier armor, 50 durability</td><td class="mono">4–5</td><td class="mono">257–343 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td>42 dmg, ammo 2 tiers under</td><td class="mono">6+</td><td class="mono">429+ ms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Illustrative values from the tier-gap model — reproduce them with your own inputs on the <a href="/ttk-calculator/">TTK calculator</a>.</p>

    <h2>What TTK doesn't tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>First-shot reliability</strong> — a 171 ms TTK means nothing if the first two shots miss.</li>
      <li><strong>Range</strong> — falloff lowers damage; enter a reduced "damage at range" percentage in the calculator.</li>
      <li><strong>Aim trajectory</strong> — headshot multipliers end fights faster than any RPM number; multipliers are input-controlled in the tool because they vary by helmet and ammo.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sources and verification</h2>
    <p>
      The tier-gap model and its rebalance history are community-documented; our standing assumptions, with
      sources and the [VERIFY] policy, live on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>. Game patches
      change these values — always check the review date before quoting numbers in an argument.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
